   254	\section{Introduction}
   255	Every Nash equilibrium (NE) in a 2-player normal form game with rational payoffs is a solution to a 
   256	linear program with rational weights \citep{roughgarden2010algorithmic}. Therefore, if 
   257	$\sigma$ is a NE then there is a NE $\tau$ with rational probability weights (henceforth we 
   258	refer to them as \emph{probabilities}) and support $\supp\sigma$. However, this conclusion 
   259	fails to hold in $n$-player games when $n\ge3$. This is because more generally, NEs are 
   260	solutions of polynomial equations and inequalities in $n$ variables and degree at most $n-1$,
   261	and these might not have solutions in the rationals. A concrete example is found in 
   262	\citet{bilo2014complexity}: a $3$-player game with integer payoffs in $\s{0,1,2,3}$, 
   263	possessing a unique NE, and its probabilities are $((x_1,1-x_1),(x_2,1-x_2),(x_3,1-x_3))$ 
   264	where
   265	$x_1=\frac{25-\sqrt{409}}{12},~x_2=\frac{13+\sqrt{409}}{60},~x_3=\frac{21-\sqrt{409}}{2}$.  
   266	These 6 probabilities are all irrational. A different known example is the 3-player poker game in 
   267	\citet{nash1950simple}.
   268	
   269	Such a result is possible for every $n\ge4$ as well but there seems to be no previously known game with this property for any such $n$.
   270	In this paper we present for every $n\ge4$ an $n$-player game $G_n$ that has 2 actions per 
   271	player and the following properties: (1) The payoffs are in $\s{0,1,2}$; (2) $G_n$ has a 
   272	unique NE; (3) that NE is fully mixed, and (4) the probability weights of this NE are all 
   273	inexpressible with radicals (henceforth we call such numbers \emph{irradicals}).  
   274	Inexpressibility with radicals is an even stronger requirement than irrationality -- it 
   275	means that these probabilities do not have closed form expressions using rational arithmetic and $m$-th roots for any integer $m$.
   276	
   277	To play a mixed NE, a player must randomly select an action from the underlying (discrete) probability distribution.
   278	Exact sampling from discrete distributions with rational probabilities is a well understood problem solved with efficient algorithms~\citep{knuth1976complexity,draper2025efficient}. There, the computations are done using integer arithmetic which be done precisely on a modern digital computer. But sampling from distributions with irrational (or irradical) probabilities using rational arithmetic is a less studied problem. Irrational numbers can only be approximated by finite integers. With current known techniques, exact sampling from a distribution with irrational weights can only be done with arbitrary precision arithmetic using a power series expansion of the probabilities~\citep{flajolet2011buffon,mendo2020simulating}, which is computationally and pragmatically more involved than sampling from distributions with rational probabilities.
   279	In our context, all the probability weights are algebraic numbers, so the sampling problem appears to require computing roots of polynomials with potentially very large degrees and coefficients (the polynomials associated with $G_5$ have degree $26$ and $10$-digit coefficients).
   280	This fact highlights a challenge that players face in this context.
   281	
   282	For example, our $4$-player game has a unique NE, 
   283	$((x_1,1-x_1),(x_2,1-x_2),(x_3,1-x_3),(x_4,1-x_4))$ such that $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ are these 
   284	roots of the polynomials
   285	\begin{align*}
   286	  P_1(z)&=7z^6-42z^5+89z^4-83z^3+40z^2-10z+1,\\
   287	  P_2(z)&=4z^6-27z^5+70z^4-79z^3+45z^2-13z+1,\\
   288	  P_3(z)&=140z^6-511z^5+701z^4-454z^3+141z^2-19z+1,\\
   289	  P_4(z)&=5z^6-44z^5+143z^4-163z^3+85z^2-21z+2,
   290	\end{align*}
   291	that are approximately
   292	$x_1\approx0.529,~x_2\approx0.846,~x_3\approx0.523,~x_4\approx0.320$. $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ 
   293	cannot be expressed with radicals because $P_1,P_2,P_3,P_4$ are all irreducible polynomials 
   294	over the rationals whose Galois groups are all $S_6$ which is not solvable 
   295	(see~\citet{artin2011algebra} for reference).
   296	
   297	To obtain the main result we first construct the mentioned $4$-player game (Proposition~\ref{prop:n=4}) and 
   298	a $5$-player game (Proposition~\ref{prop:n=5}) with a unique NE whose probabilities are 
   299	irradical. We then use these two games to construct such a game for any $n\ge6$ 
   300	(Proposition~\ref{prop:n>=6}). More specifically, for $n\ge6$ we juxtapose independent copies of 
   301	the $4$-player and $5$-player games, and to one of the game copies possibly attach 2 
   302	additional players who force each other to mimic the mixed strategy of one player in the game 
   303	copy.
   304	
   305	We complement this result by showing that every $2\times2\times2$ game with integer payoffs 
   306	has a NE whose probabilities can be expressed using only rationals and square roots of 
   307	rationals (Proposition~\ref{prop:n=3-sqrt}).
   308	We also present a simple $3$-player game with integer payoffs in only $\s{0,1,2}$ whose 
   309	unique NE has only irrational probabilities (Proposition~\ref{prop:n=3}). This game is 
   310	simpler than that of \citet{bilo2014complexity}.
   311	Its unique NE $((x_1,1-x_1),(x_2,1-x_2),(x_3,1-x_3))$ where
   312	$x_1=\frac{7-\sqrt{13}}{6},~x_2=\frac{7+\sqrt{13}}{18},x_3=\frac{-3+\sqrt{13}}{2}$.
   313	These are approximately $x_1\approx0.566,~x_2\approx0.589,~x_3\approx0.303$.
   314	
   315	Note that given such a $3$-player game it is easy to extend it to an $n$-player game where 
   316	\emph{some} of the players need to play mixed actions with irrational probabilities in 
   317	every NE -- simply add players with one dominating action each. But then only the first 3 
   318	players will need to play NE strategies with irrational probabilities. We are interested in 
   319	forcing \emph{all} players to play NE strategies with irrational probabilities. For this 
   320	reason we are interested only in fully mixed equilibria.
   321	
   322	It is also possible to use such a $3$-player game to generate $n$-player games for every 
   323	$n\ge5$ where all players need to play NE strategies with irrational probabilities. This is 
   324	achieved by applying our results in Section~\ref{sec:ext} to such a $3$-player game.  
   325	However, this approach alone leaves unresolved the case of 4 players, and all the probabilities in the other cases will not be irradical.
   326	
   327	There are some related results in the literature. First is the characterization of 
   328	\citet{kreps1981finite} of (mixed) strategy profiles which can be the unique NEs of a 
   329	suitable normal form game. In particular, any fully mixed strategy profile for $n$ players 
   330	with 2 actions each is the unique NE of some $n$-player game (with 2 actions per player).  
   331	However, the payoffs in the constructed game will 
   332	involve the probabilities in that strategy profile. In particular, if the probabilities are 
   333	irrational then the payoffs found will be irrational. Here we are looking for games with 
   334	integer payoffs, since these are simpler to store on a computer than irrational payoffs.
   335	
   336	Another result is by \citet{bubelis1979equilibria} who constructs for every algebraic 
   337	number $\alpha$ a $3$-player game with integer payoffs such that $\alpha$ is the payoff to 
   338	some player in the NE of the game. However, in the constructed game player~$3$ has the fully mixed 
   339	uniform distribution (which is comprised of rational probabilities) as an equilibrium 
   340	strategy.
   341	
   342	Another related result is the universality of NEs by \citet{datta2003universality}. This 
   343	result asserts that every semialgebraic set is isomorphic to the set of NEs of some game.  
   344	However, an isomorphism in this context only 
   345	preserves the topological structure of the semialgebraic set rather than its exact members.  
   346	In particular, in \citet{datta2003universality}, a semialgebraic set consisting of a single 
   347	point with irrational entries will be mapped to a NE profile with rational probabilities.
   348	
   349	\paragraph{Paper organization} In Section~\ref{sec:defs} we define the setup and provide 
   350	algebra background. In Section~\ref{sec:3} we present our results for $3$-player, 
   351	$2\times2\times2$ games. In Section~\ref{sec:4} and Section~\ref{sec:5} we present the 
   352	$4$-player game and $5$-player game. In Section~\ref{sec:ext} we use the latter two games 
   353	to construct the remaining $n$-player games for every $n\ge6$.
   354	
