   761	
   762	  Therefore the only NEs are fully mixed. This means that $x\in\NE(G_4)$ satisfies 
   763	  $f_i(x)=0$ for every $i$. We solve this now.
   764	
   765	  Let $F=\s{f_1,f_2,f_3,f_4}$. It is a set of polynomials in the polynomial ring 
   766	  $\Q[x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4]$. We begin by finding the Gr\"obner basis $G=\s{g_1,g_2,g_3,g_4}$ of 
   767	  the ideal $\langle F\rangle$, with the monomial ordering generated lexicographically by 
   768	  $x_1\succ x_2\succ x_3\succ x_4$, given by
   769	  \begin{align*}
   770	    g_1&=5x_4^6-44x_4^5+143x_4^4-163x_4^3+85x_4^2-21x_4+2,\\
   771	    g_2&=28x_3-1465x_4^5+12302x_4^4-36947x_4^3+32897x_4^2-11699x_4+1447,\\
   772	    g_3&=4x_2+255x_4^5-2094x_4^4+6053x_4^3-4687x_4^2+1401x_4-149,\\
   773	    g_4&=7x_1+5x_4^5-39x_4^4+104x_4^3-59x_4^2+26x_4-9.
   774	  \end{align*}
   775	  These were found using Mathematica~\citep{Mathematica}.
   776	
   777	  Now we compute the solutions of $\forall i~g_i=0$ to find those of $\forall i~f_i=0$.  
   778	  First, we approximate the 6 roots of $g_1$ numerically:
   779	  \begin{align*}
   780	    r_1&\approx0.3200651976,~r_2\approx0.4231894254,\\
   781	    r_3&\approx0.4135410939-0.09991760306i,~r_4\approx0.4135410939+0.09991760306i,\\
   782	    r_5&\approx3.614831595-1.802444362i,~r_6\approx3.614831595+1.802444362i.
   783	  \end{align*}
   784	  $x_4\in(0,1)$ so $x_4\in\s{r_1,r_2}$. If $x_4=r_2$ then for every $r'_2\in[0.42,0.43]$, $g_2(r'_2,x_3)=0$ iff $x_3>1$ -- a contradiction to $x_3<1$.
   785	  Therefore $x_4=r_1$.\footnote{See \ref{app:sturm} for how to approximate the roots of $g_1$ using exact arithmetic without relying on the problematic floating point arithmetic.} Using the equalities $g_2=g_3=g_4=0$ we arrive at the approximations
   786	  \begin{align*}
   787	    x_1\approx0.529270752820,&~x_2\approx0.846414728986,\\
   788	    x_3\approx0.523440476515,&~x_4\approx0.320065197645.
   789	  \end{align*}
   790	  
   791	  We now show that $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ are irradical.
   792	  First, for each of the 3 monomial orderings generated lexicographically by $x_2\succ 
   793	  x_3\succ x_4\succ x_1$, $x_3\succ x_4\succ x_1\succ x_2$ and $x_4\succ x_1\succ x_2\succ 
   794	  x_3$, we find corresponding Gr\"obner bases, in addition to the 
   795	  one that we found initially. From them we collect the univariate polynomials
   796	  \begin{align*}
   797	    P_1(y)&=7y^6-42y^5+89y^4-83y^3+40y^2-10y+1,\\
   798	    P_2(y)&=4y^6-27y^5+70y^4-79y^3+45y^2-13y+1,\\
   799	    P_3(y)&=140y^6-511y^5+701y^4-454y^3+141y^2-19y+1,\\
   800	    P_4(y)&=5y^6-44y^5+143y^4-163y^3+85y^2-21y+2.
   801	  \end{align*}
   802	  which we further know satisfy $P_i(x_i)=0$. These exist by the elimination theorem for 
   803	  Gr\"obner bases \citep{cox1997ideals}.
   804	
   805	  Now, we use Murty's criterion~\citep{murty2002prime} to show that $P_1,P_2,P_3,P_4$ are 
   806	  all irreducible over $\Q$.
   807	  \begin{enumerate}
   808	    \item $P_1$ is irreducible because $P_1(18)=167595301$ is prime and the $H$ value of 
   809	      $P_1$ is $\frac{89}{7}\approx12.714$.
   810	    \item $P_2$ is irreducible because $P_2(28)=1504207909$ is prime and the $H$ value of 
   811	      $P_2$ is $\frac{79}{4}=19.75$.
   812	    \item $P_3$ is irreducible because $P_3(11)=175397399$ is prime and the $H$ value of 
   813	      $P_3$ is $\frac{701}{140}\approx5.007$.
   814	    \item $P_4$ is irreducible because $P_4(39)=13945135583$ is prime and the $H$ value of 
   815	      $P_4$ is $\frac{163}{5}=32.6$.
   816	  \end{enumerate}
   817	  
   818	  As a result, $P_i$ is the minimal polynomial of $x_i$ (up to normalizing the leading 
   819	  coefficient of $P_i$), so $x_i$ is irrational for every $i$. Now, it can be verified that 
   820	  the Galois group of $P_i$ is $S_6$ for every $i\in\s{1,2,3,4}$ (we used 
   821	  Magma~\citep{MR1484478}). Therefore $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ are all irradical.
   822	  This concludes the proof.
   823	\end{proof}
