  1067	\section{Six players and up}\label{sec:ext}
  1068	
  1069	Here we use the constructions of Section~\ref{sec:4} and Section~\ref{sec:5} to construct 
  1070	games $G_n$ for every $n\ge6$ with the same property as $G_4$ and $G_5$. This completes the 
  1071	picture in terms of the (general worst case) inexpressibility of NEs with radicals.
  1072	
  1073	First, we use the notion of \emph{product of games}:
  1074	
  1075	\begin{definition}
  1076	  Given are games $H^1,H^2$ such that $H^j$ has $n_j$ players, the pure action 
  1077	  profiles $A^j$ and the payoff functions $u^j_1,\ldots,u^j_{n_j}$. Define the 
  1078	  \emph{product game} $H^1\times H^2$ as follows:
  1079	  \begin{itemize}
  1080	    \item there are $n_1+n_2$ players,
  1081	    \item the set of pure action profiles is $A^1\times A^2$,
  1082	    \item the payoff functions are $u_i(a^1,a^2)=u^1_i(a^1)$ if $i\in[n_1]$ and 
  1083	      $u^2_i(a^2)$ if $i\in\s{n_1+1,\ldots,n_1+n_2}$.
  1084	  \end{itemize}
  1085	  If $H_1=H_2$, also write $H_1\times H_2=H_1^{\times2}$, and generalize the notation to a 
  1086	  product of $q$ copies of $H_1$ by $H_1^{\times q}$.
  1087	\end{definition}
  1088	
  1089	This operation is associative, therefore $H_1^{\times q}$ is well defined. Here is a property of $H^1\times H^2$ that follows by definition:
  1090	
  1091	\begin{lemma}\label{lmm:product-ne}
  1092	  $\NE(H^1\times H^2)=\NE(H^1)\times\NE(H^2)$.
  1093	\end{lemma}
  1094	
  1095	Using this lemma, for every $n=4n_1+5n_2$ where $n_1,n_2\in\N$ are not both $0$, the game $G_4^{\times n_1}\times G_5^{\times n_2}$ has a unique NE and its probabilities are all irradical (see Proposition~\ref{prop:n>=6} below). However, these constructions do not cover the cases 
  1096	$n=6,7,11$. To fill these holes, we now show how to add 2 players to each of  $G_4$ and 
  1097	$G_5$ while maintaining their desired properties (a unique NE, whose probabilities are 
  1098	irradical).
  1099	
  1100	Define the $2\times2\times2$ game $H_3$:
  1101	\begin{figure}[H]
  1102	\centering
  1103	\begin{align*}
  1104	  \left[\begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
  1105	    0&0&0&0&1&1\\
  1106	    0&0&1&0&1&1\\
  1107	    0&1&0&0&1&0\\
  1108	    0&1&1&0&0&1\\
  1109	    1&0&0&0&0&1\\
  1110	    1&0&1&0&0&0\\
  1111	    1&1&0&0&1&0\\
  1112	    1&1&1&0&0&0
  1113	  \end{array}\right]
  1114	\end{align*}
  1115	\caption{The game $H_3$}
  1116	\label{fig:H3}
  1117	\end{figure}
  1118	
  1119	Here is the main property of interest of this game:
  1120	
  1121	\begin{proposition}\label{prop:H3-NE}
  1122	  Let $x=((x_1,1-x_1),(x_2,1-x_2),(x_3,1-x_3))$ such that $x_1\in(0,1)$. Then $x\in\NE(H_3)$ iff $x_1=x_2=x_3$.
  1123	\end{proposition}
  1124	\begin{proof}
  1125	  First, the polynomials $f_1,f_2,f_3$ from Equation~\ref{eq:polys} of $H_3$ are
  1126	  \begin{align*}
  1127	    f_1(x_2,x_3)=0,~f_2(x_1,x_3)=x_1-x_3,~f_3(x_1,x_2)=x_2-x_1.
  1128	  \end{align*}
  1129	
  1130	  By definition, for every $x_1\in(0,1)$ setting $x_1=x_2=x_3$ means that $f_1(x)=f_2(x)=f_3(x)=0$, meaning that $x\in\NE(H_3)$. Now we show that if $x_1\in(0,1)$ and $x\in\NE(H_3)$ then $x_1=x_2=x_3$.
  1131	
  1132	  We use Lemma~\ref{lmm:ne-poly-cond}. $f_1=0$ so $x_1$ can take on 
  1133	  any value, regardless of the values of $x_2,x_3$. Assume that $x_1\in(0,1)$.
  1134	  Suppose 
  1135	  for contradiction that $x_2=0$. Then $f_3=-x_1<0$, so $x_3=0$ so $f_2=x_1>0$ so $x_2=1$ 
  1136	  -- a contradiction. Similarly, if $x_2=1$, then $f_3=1-x_1>0$ so $x_3=1$ so $f_2=x_1-1<0$ 
  1137	  so $x_2=0$ -- again a contradiction. Therefore $x_2\in(0,1)$ so $f_2=0$ so $x_3=x_1$ so $f_3=0$ so $x_1=x_2=x_3$.
  1138	\end{proof}
  1139	
  1140	Next we define a variation of the product game with a small overlap in the players.  
  1141	
  1142	\begin{definition}
  1143	  Let $G$ be a game such that
  1144	  \begin{itemize}
  1145	    \item there are $n$ players,
  1146	    \item each player has $2$ pure actions,
  1147	    \item the payoffs are given by functions $u_1,\ldots,u_n$,
  1148	    \item there is a unique NE, and this NE is fully mixed.
  1149	  \end{itemize}
  1150	  Define the game $G\circ H_3$ as follows:
  1151	  \begin{itemize}
  1152	    \item there are $n+2$ players,
  1153	    \item each player has $2$ pure actions,
  1154	    \item the payoff functions are given by $u_i(a,a_{n+1},a_{n+2})=u_i(a)$ for $i\in[n]$, 
  1155	      and for $i\in\s{n+1,n+2}$ the payoff is defined as 
  1156	      $u_i(a,a_{n+1},a_{n+2})=u^{H_3}_{i-(n-1)}(a_n,a_{n+1},a_{n+2})$ (as defined in 
  1157	      Figure~\ref{fig:H3}).
  1158	  \end{itemize}
  1159	\end{definition}
  1160	
  1161	The most important thing about this operation $\circ H_3$ is that it is comptaible with 
  1162	$G_4$ and $G_5$. The key property of $G\circ H_3$ is as follows:
  1163	
  1164	\begin{lemma}\label{lmm:circ-ne}
  1165	  If $\NE(G)=\s{((x_1,1-x_1),\ldots,(x_n,1-x_n))}$ and $x_n\in(0,1)$ then
  1166	  \begin{align*}
  1167	    \NE(G\circ H_3)=\s{((x_1,1-x_1),\ldots,(x_n,1-x_n),(x_n,1-x_n),(x_n,1-x_n))}.
  1168	  \end{align*}
  1169	\end{lemma}
  1170	\begin{proof}
  1171	  By definition. Observe that a NE of $G\circ H_3$, restricted to players $n,n+1,n+2$, is a 
  1172	  NE to $H_3$, because the payoff of the first player in $H_3$ (meaning player $n$ in $G\circ H_3$) is constant. Since player $n$ plays $(x_n,1-x_n)$ in the only NE of $G$, player $n$ will need to play it in any NE of $G\circ H_3$. Therefore we 
  1173	  can invoke Proposition~\ref{prop:H3-NE} here to get the desired result.
  1174	\end{proof}
  1175	
  1176	Now the games $G_n$ for $n\ge6$ follow. Let $n\ge6$. Write $n=4q+r$ where $1\le q\in\N$ and 
  1177	$r\in\s{0,1,2,3}$. Define
  1178	\begin{align}
  1179	  G_n=\begin{cases}
  1180	    G_4^{\times q}&r=0,\\
  1181	    G_4^{\times q-1}\times G_5&r=1,\\
  1182	    G_4^{\times q-1}\times(G_4\circ H_3)&r=2,\\
  1183	    G_4^{\times q-1}\times(G_5\circ H_3)&r=3.
  1184	  \end{cases}
  1185	\end{align}
  1186	
  1187	\begin{proposition}\label{prop:n>=6}
  1188	  For every $n\ge6$, the game $G_n$ has a unique NE, and the probabilities in this NE are 
  1189	  all irradical.
  1190	\end{proposition}
  1191	\begin{proof}
  1192	  Let $n=4q+r$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:n=4}, Proposition~\ref{prop:n=5} and 
  1193	  Lemma~\ref{lmm:circ-ne} we obtain that $G_4\circ H_3$ and $G_5\circ H_3$ both have a 
  1194	  unique NE, and that NE is irradical in both games. Now, for any $n\ge6$, $G_n$ is a 
  1195	  product of games which all have a unique NE, and that NE is irradical in all those games. Therefore each $G_n$ has a unique NE, and its probabilities are all irradical.
  1196	\end{proof}
